Consider this statement about a function  $ f(x) $:

 $$ \left(*\right)\mathrm{If}\left(f(x)\right)^{2}\leq1\mathrm{~for~all}-1\leq x\leq1\mathrm{~then}\int_{-1}^{1}\left(f(x)\right)^{2}\mathrm{d}x\leq\int_{-1}^{1}f(x)\mathrm{d}x $$ 

Which one of the following functions provides a counterexample to  $ * $?

A \quad f(x) = x + \frac{1}{2}

B \quad f(x) = x - \frac{1}{2}

C \quad f(x) = x + x^{3}

D \quad f(x) = x - x^{3}

E \quad f(x) = x^{2} + x^{4}

F \quad f(x) = x^{2} - x^{4}

(*) If $f(x)$ is an integer for every integer $x$, then $f'(x)$ is an integer for every integer $x$.

Which one of the following is a counterexample to (*)?

$$

\begin{aligned}

\mathbf{A} & \quad f(x) = \frac{x^3 + x + 1}{4} \\

\mathbf{B} & \quad f(x) = \frac{x^4 + x^2 + x}{2} \\

\mathbf{C} & \quad f(x) = \frac{x^4 + x^3 + x^2 + x}{2} \\

\mathbf{D} & \quad f(x) = \frac{x^4 + 2x^3 + x^2}{4}

\end{aligned}

$$

